// Framing and graphics-transform boxes: \fbox / \framebox / \colorbox / \fcolorbox,
// \rotatebox / \scalebox / \reflectbox / \resizebox, and \raisebox.
//
// Render with:  texish scripts/framebox-demo.script -p letter

\use{logos}

\def headline {\italic{Framing & transforms} \hfil page \the\pageno}
\def footline {\hfil — \the\pageno — \hfil}

\font lmroman 12 regular

\bold{\TeXish{} framing and transform boxes}

These boxes wrap a piece of typeset content and either frame it or transform it,
flowing inline like any other box.

\bold{Framing}

A framed word: \fbox{framed} sits in running text, padded by \fbox{fboxsep} inside
a rule. A filled background: \colorbox{yellow}{highlighted} draws no rule, only the
fill. Both together: \fcolorbox{darkred}{mistyrose}{boxed and filled} draws a frame
in one colour over a fill in another.

\bold{Rotation}

Text can be turned about its baseline: \rotatebox{30}{thirty degrees} rises to the
left, while \rotatebox{90}{ninety} stands on end and \rotatebox{180}{upside down}
reads the other way. The line grows to hold the rotated box.

\bold{Scaling and reflection}

Scale a word up — \scalebox{2}{big} — or stretch one axis only with
\scalebox{1}[2]{tall}. Mirror it left to right with \reflectbox{reflected}. Fit a
word to an exact width, aspect preserved: \resizebox{1in}{!}{one inch wide}.

\bold{Raising}

The \raisebox{6pt}{baseline} of a box can be \raisebox{-4pt}{shifted} up or down,
and unlike \fbox{plain} raising, the box's own height follows it.
